CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=8a0183a7-83184018-0184-87170e31-00006277:Hinz\, Nic
 olle
CREATED:20261117T012756Z
DESCRIPTION:In living systems\, the morphology of the body is tightly coup
 led to the robustness and speed of the sense-assess-respond feedback loop
  for optimal flight control\, maneuverability and many other functions. T
 he concept of "embodiment" emphasizes the significant role that physical 
 shape\, mechanical compliance\, and multi-functionality can play in facil
 itating feedback responses that point toward intelligent behavior and con
 trol. Using the mathematical framework of reservoir computing\, it is pos
 sible to harness these intrinsic mechanical behaviors and dynamics from t
 he loading environment as a computational resource. In this presentation\
 , we will evaluate the information processing capacity of several physica
 l reservoir computing systems\, including a fluid structure interaction m
 odel\, a magnetomechanical resonator\, and an optomechanical spring netwo
 rk. We characterize the relationship between system nonlinearity and rese
 rvoir computing performance using a frequency content metric. We then exp
 loit the ability to tune the optical nonlinearity through controlling the
  amount of fiber pre-compression and the reference leakage position. A fe
 edback error signal combined with a local learning rule changes the nonli
 near transformation applied by the optical fiber\, driving down the error
  without retraining the output weights. Together\, this work motivates th
 e exploration of design strategies to embed sense-assess-respond feedback
  loops at the material- and structure-level for intelligent and autonomou
 s systems.
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20261117T012756Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261123T120000
LAST-MODIFIED:20261117T012756Z
